<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Space Race</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1957">1957</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Space Race was the competition between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, from the late 1950s to the mid-1970s, to achieve the first feats of spaceflight. The Soviet Union launched the first artificial satellite, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sputnik-1">Sputnik 1</a>, in 1957 and the first person in space, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-gagarin">Yuri Gagarin</a>, in 1961; the United States answered with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> programme, which landed astronauts on the Moon in July 1969. The rivalry, a contest of prestige in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, eased with the joint Apollo–Soyuz mission of 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/space-race.jpg" alt="Space Race" width="330" height="270" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NSSDC, NASA [1] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sputnik_asm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Space Race grew out of the ballistic missile arms race between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Launching satellites and people required large missiles such as Wernher von Braun's A-4, known as the V-2, developed by Nazi Germany. After the war both powers took custody of German rocket assets. The United States recruited von Braun and most of his team in Operation Paperclip, and they later developed the Redstone rocket at Huntsville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>. On 29 July 1955 the United States announced that it intended to launch small Earth circling satellites for the International Geophysical Year, and five days later the Soviet Union said it would also launch one in the near future.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Soviet launch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sputnik-1">Sputnik 1</a> on 4 October 1957 caused what the Western public called the Sputnik crisis. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> launched Explorer 1 on 31 January 1958, and its instruments helped prove the existence of the Van Allen radiation belt. The Soviet Luna 2 impacted the Moon on 14 September 1959, and Luna 3 sent back pictures of its far side the next month. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-gagarin">Yuri Gagarin</a>'s orbital flight in Vostok 1 on 12 April 1961 led President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a>, on 25 May 1961, to ask Congress to commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the end of the decade.</p><p>Both countries built super heavy-lift rockets. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> developed the Saturn V, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> 11 landed on the Moon in July 1969 with Neil Armstrong, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-collins">Michael Collins</a> and Buzz Aldrin. The Soviet N1 rocket did not succeed and was cancelled, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> turned to the Salyut space stations and to robotic landings on Venus and Mars. The United States landed five more Apollo crews on the Moon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Détente followed. In May 1972 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nixon">Richard Nixon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-brezhnev">Leonid Brezhnev</a> agreed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>–Soyuz Test Project, and in July 1975 a US astronaut crew and a Soviet cosmonaut crew met in Earth orbit. Many observers consider this the final act of the race, though competition was only gradually replaced by cooperation. In 1993 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> agreed on the Shuttle–Mir and International Space Station programmes.</p><p>Historians disagree on who won. Many see the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> landings as a decisive American victory, but Asif A. Siddiqi stresses that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>'s early and sustained robotic exploration of the Moon and planets shows a broader rivalry than one event. Walter A. McDougall describes space exploration as a demonstration of each superpower's political and technological systems.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Space Race” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its radio beeps, picked up worldwide, shocked the West and launched the <b>Space Race</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-4">4 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
